      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The disaster genre saw a significant revival in the 1990s with films like <a href=\"/movie/664\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"664\">Twister</a> (1996), <a href=\"/movie/10357\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10357\">Volcano</a> (1997), and <a href=\"/movie/8656\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8656\">Deep Impact</a> (1998) updating the formula with CGI spectacle. Poseidon follows this trend but focuses more on intimate survival horror than epic destruction. The confined spaces and rising water create sustained tension similar to <a href=\"/movie/11228\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11228\">Daylight</a> (1996) and <a href=\"/movie/8914\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8914\">Deep Blue Sea</a> (1999), where characters must navigate increasingly dangerous environments while their safe zones steadily shrink. SUBHEAD: Visual Effects Evolution and Maritime Disasters"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Poseidon's effects work builds on advances made in water simulation since <a href=\"/movie/2756\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"2756\">The Abyss</a> (1989). The capsizing sequence particularly showcases how far digital effects had come, though some critics felt it lacked the practical effects charm of the 1972 original. The film sits alongside other modern maritime thrillers like <a href=\"/movie/83\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"83\">Open Water</a> (2003) and <a href=\"/movie/10534\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10534\">White Squall</a> (1996), proving the ocean remains one of cinema's most effective sources of natural danger."
      }
